   "result": "I added a 3D view to `index.html` that puts pillars on each station. Moving the slider to a different time changes their heights. I checked it in headless Chrome: at 08시, 서울역's 하차 pillar is the tallest, which matches the data (274,196 that hour). I only looked at screenshots, though. Dragging the slider, the play button, mouse-hover tooltips and orbiting the view have not been tried by hand yet.\n\n- **Placement:** the 3D view sits above the existing charts, which are unchanged. Station coordinates aren't in the data, so I typed in approximate positions for the five stations.\n- **Pillars:** each station has two side by side, 승차 in blue and 하차 in orange. Heights use the same scale as the 2D charts below, so you can compare across stations and times.\n- **Slider:** it runs from 04시 to 03시 the next day and starts at 08시. Moving it makes the heights change smoothly. The play button steps forward one hour every 0.8 seconds and loops.\n- **Tooltip:** hovering over a pillar shows that station's 승차 and 하차 counts for the current hour, styled like the existing tooltip.\n\nOn a 375px-wide screen, 서울역 was cut off the edge of the 3D view at first. I fixed it by moving the camera back further on narrow screens, and now all five stations fit. At 375px the page also doesn't scroll sideways.\n\nTwo things to know when viewing it:\n- The page now has to be served, not opened directly: run `python3 -m http.server 8000` in this folder and open http://127.0.0.1:8000. This is what the README already recommends.\n- It also needs an internet connection the first time, because the 3D library (Three.js) is loaded from a CDN (jsDelivr).",
