   "result": "I added a \"3D 지도\" section at the top of `index.html` that stands 3D pillars at each station; moving the time slider changes their heights. I loaded the page in headless Chrome and it renders: the pillars, route line and labels all appear (screenshot taken at the 08시 slot).\n\n- **Pillars:** each station gets two pillars side by side, blue for boardings (승차) and orange for alightings (하차), using the same colors as the existing charts. All stations and time slots share one height scale, so heights are directly comparable.\n- **Time slider:** it covers 04시 through 03시 the next day and starts at 08시. When you move it, the pillars grow or shrink smoothly to the new height. A **▶ 재생** button steps forward one hour every 0.9 seconds.\n- **Labels:** a label above each pillar pair shows the station name and that hour's boarding and alighting counts.\n- **Camera:** drag to rotate and use the mouse wheel to zoom. A grey line connects the five Line 1 stations in route order.\n\nThe data has no coordinates, so I typed in rough latitude/longitude values for the five stations myself (`STATIONS` in `index.html`). If you add more stations, you'll need to add their coordinates there; stations without one are left off the 3D map.\n\nThree.js r160 loads from the jsDelivr CDN, so the page needs an internet connection. The page also fetches its data file, so open it through `python3 -m http.server` rather than by double-clicking the file. I added these notes to `README.md`.",
